[Music]  he sold more than 170 million books  including one of our all-time favorites  the pillars of the earth  this week ken foley is out with a  prequel the evening and the morning  an epic tale about the world emerging  from the dark ages  it is once again signature fallette full  of history  architecture scandal and memorable  characters  from outside london we spoke to falet  about the evening and the morning  as well as the groundbreaking work he's  done in years past  sometimes people say i had given up on  reading  and i hadn't read a novel for 20 years  and then somebody gave me the pillars of  the the earth and they said you've  really got to read this book you've  really got to read this book  and i enjoyed it so much that it's got  me reading again  ken follett speaks with grandiosity  about pillars of the earth  which was later turned into a tv  miniseries i don't think kindly to  priests who steal  god doesn't take climate bishops who lie  but so do legions of its readers  who find its combination of storytelling  in history  a magical experience  now felit has traveled across the uk to  put together  the evening in the morning his prequel  to pillars  which opens with a savage viking  conquest in the year 997.  there's a mystique about vikings i think  that people are really fascinated by  but you make clear very quickly in this  book that these these are not the good  guys  people think the vikings are sexy and i  really don't  they were murderers thieves and  slavers and uh they were just the the  worst people in western europe  to write the evening and the morning  falet studied the bayou tapestry  a sort of comic book version of what  life was like at the end  of the dark ages they didn't have dogs  you wanted to go  aboard a ship you hitched up the skirts  of your tunic  around your waist and you walked through  the shallow water  if the information wasn't available he  got creative  for example in the evening in the  morning i did  everything i could to find out what  underwear people wore  in the dark ages or perhaps none but  perhaps some  and nobody knew so i made something up  and  and that's what you have to do  as usual in his fiction falette has  embraced strong characters  from all sides some books appeal more to  men and some books appeal more to women  one of the things about your books is  that i think there's been it's  it's always cut across those lines and  that was the goal i  i assume with this as well well  absolutely  and um when i was writing my first  successful book which was a wartime spy  story card eye of the needle  uh halfway through the planning process  i changed the main  character from a man to a woman because  everybody's read you know ten thrillers  about  two guys and it made the story much more  interesting  a decade after eye of the needle pillars  became an even bigger publishing  phenomenon  in part by leaving almost nothing out of  bounds  you've never shied away from the carnal  aspects  of life in your books why is that it's  partly because i and my readers like the  carnal aspects of life but there is a  literary reason as well  there's often in a a novel  there's a love story in which two people  are crazy about one another but they  can't be together  and whatever the reason that's the story  if two people meet and fall in love and  get married and live happily ever after  it's not a story is it  but if you have had these two people for  two or three hundred pages looking at  one another over a crowded room  wishing they could be alone together  wishing that they could kiss but not  being allowed to kiss  and then finally comes the moment  when they can be together they're alone  together they can do  all the things that they've been  dreaming about  for the last 300 pages then it's only  fair to the reader that we should go  with them just for a little way at least  into their paradise there was no king  there was no queen there is no law in  this land  falet's epic ancient tales have never  been easy to transfer to television  but that hasn't stopped filmmakers from  trying when do we see the evening in the  morning  on a screen well wouldn't that be nice  it would be an expensive show to make  but uh  i can just see it it opens with a big  dramatic  action scene i i'm hoping hollywood will  love it  the evening and the morning he is fun to  talk to you right now i love him  by the way even just describing the  carnal nature i was like  what yeah i mean like i was there it's  crazy he's fun to talk to he's also a  master storyteller  and the books are really tough to put  down he is a character  he really truly is and we thank you for  sitting down with us such a great story

